---
import FocusedServicePage from '../components/FocusedServicePage.astro';
const data={
 slug:'international-seo-services',name:'International SEO Services',metaTitle:'International SEO Services | Market Expansion | Bright Forge',description:'International SEO for businesses selling in more than one English-speaking country: market research, site structure, hreflang and separate reports per country.',
 heroTitle:'International SEO That Gives <span>Each Market a Clear Role</span>',cta:'Discuss Your Market Expansion',
 intro:['International SEO is for a business that sells in more than one country and wants each country\'s customers to find the right version of its website. Copying the site and changing the currency does not achieve that. We decide with you which markets justify their own pages, how the site should be structured, and how Google is told which page belongs to which country.','You receive a market and query map, a page owner for every search that matters, the hreflang and canonical specification for your developer, and a report for each country. Our Philippines-based team supports English-language expansion across markets including the UK, US, Australia and New Zealand. This is a different service from hiring an overseas agency: if you are a business in one of those countries looking for an SEO team, the country pages linked below are the place to start.'],
 proofTitle:'Three Regional Storefronts for One Brand,',proofHighlight:'With Published Work to Review',
 proofBody:['Our published Speediance work covers the UK, Australian and New Zealand storefronts of one fitness equipment brand, selling the same product family to three country audiences. The published comparisons report the Australian store\'s organic clicks rising from 6,550 to 10,900 and the New Zealand store\'s from 927 to 2,570, each over six months against the previous six. The UK page is a project review that describes the work for that market. Each case should be read in its own market and reporting period.','A reported increase in clicks does not establish that one tag or technical change caused it, and the Australian comparison shows clicks rising while average position slipped from 24.9 to 28.1, which is why we read results at query level. We cite the work to show the type of business and regional problem we have handled. Your own diagnosis determines the next recommendation.'],
 proofLinks:[{href:'/case-studies/speediance-uk-fitness-seo/',label:'Speediance UK'},{href:'/case-studies/speediance-au-fitness-seo/',label:'Speediance Australia'},{href:'/case-studies/speediance-nz-fitness-seo/',label:'Speediance New Zealand'}],
 sections:[
  {id:'market-and-page-plan',eyebrow:'Before New Pages',title:'Market Research That Decides',highlight:'Which Countries Earn Their Own Pages',body:['We begin with what you can actually sell or deliver in each country. Availability, service boundaries, currency, buying questions and competition all change the search opportunity. A large keyword estimate does not justify a new market on its own, and it never justifies a duplicate page.'],rows:[{title:'Country-Specific Research',body:'We compare the competitors and queries that appear in the intended country, in the words its buyers use. Searcher location is kept separate from words in the query, so overseas interest in your home-market service is not mistaken for local-market demand.'},{title:'Existing Page Ownership',body:'We map which pages already meet the intent before proposing additions. The output sorts every target into one of three: an existing owner to strengthen, a regional variant with a real purpose, or an opportunity to defer.'},{title:'Commercial Priorities',body:'We prioritize pages connected to a viable offer and a working inquiry or purchase path. Translation, stock, legal requirements and delivery constraints need resolving before a campaign can promise to serve a new audience.'}],links:[{href:'/keyword-research-services/',label:'Keyword and competitor research'},{href:'/seo-audit-services/',label:'Existing-site audit'}]},
  {id:'regional-architecture',eyebrow:'Technical Ownership',title:'Site Structure, hreflang and Canonicals',highlight:'Specified Page by Page',body:['We review the country URL structure, internal links, canonical relationships and reciprocal hreflang annotations where regional equivalents exist. A missing return tag, a canonical pointing at the wrong storefront and a genuine duplicate are three different faults, and each needs its own fix.','The structure question has no universal answer. Country folders on one domain are easier to maintain and share authority. A country-code domain can be a strong market signal, and separate domains make sense when the businesses are run separately. Moving domains introduces its own risk, so we compare the options against your existing visibility and operating needs. We do not prescribe a migration as a shortcut.'],rows:[{title:'One Site or Several Stores',body:'We document which pages are equivalent, which offer different products and which should remain unique. Regional tags belong only on true equivalents. Two unrelated pages do not become alternates because they share a service label.'},{title:'Implementation and Verification',body:'The handover states the affected URLs, the recommended changes and the checks. After release we inspect the live pages and their regional links. Implementation success and later search performance are reported separately.'}],links:[{href:'/technical-seo-services-philippines/',label:'Technical SEO implementation'},{href:'/website-migration-services/',label:'Migration risk and launch support'}]},
  {id:'market-content-and-measurement',eyebrow:'Content and Measurement',title:'Local Content and Reporting,',highlight:'Kept Separate for Each Country',body:['We adapt English-language content to the market: the actual offer, spelling and terminology, currency and units, delivery terms, contact details, purchasing questions and supporting proof. Country-name substitutions and fabricated local offices do not help buyers decide. Where a market needs local-language production, that work is separately scoped with qualified resources rather than assumed to be part of this service.','Measurement combines country-filtered Search Console data, agreed country queries and page ownership, reported per country so that one market\'s growth cannot hide another\'s decline. We separate branded demand, broad discovery and commercial pages. Inquiries or sales are reported where reliable tracking connects them; impressions are never presented as revenue.'],links:[{href:'/ecommerce-seo-services/',label:'Multi-market ecommerce support'},{href:'/seo-services-new-zealand/',label:'SEO for New Zealand businesses'},{href:'/seo-services-uk/',label:'SEO for UK businesses'},{href:'/seo-services-usa/',label:'SEO for US businesses'},{href:'/seo-services-australia/',label:'SEO for Australian businesses'}]},
  {id:'multi-market-case',eyebrow:'Published Multi-Market Work',title:'One Brand, Three Countries:',highlight:'Speediance Results by Market',body:['Speediance sells the same smart home gym range through separate storefronts for the UK, Australia and New Zealand. Each store has its own campaign and its own published review, and the figures below are reported per country. They are Search Console figures for each storefront, measuring search clicks and impressions rather than orders.'],rows:[{title:'Australia',body:'Organic clicks rose from 6,550 to 10,900 and impressions from 279,000 to 339,000 over six months against the previous six. Click-through rate moved from 2.4% to 3.2%. Average position slipped from 24.9 to 28.1, which the case study flags for query-level review.'},{title:'New Zealand',body:'Organic clicks rose from 927 to 2,570 and impressions from 46,600 to 85,800 over six months against the previous six. Average position improved from 31.3 to 25.0 on the storefront at speediance.nz.'},{title:'United Kingdom',body:'The published page for speediance.co.uk is a project review. It describes the work for UK buyers comparing compact home gyms and reports no traffic figures, so we quote none here.'},{title:'Set-Ups We Handle',body:'We handle separate country domains, as in this case, regional folders on one domain, and one shared catalog serving several countries. On the platform side we work with Shopify, WooCommerce and WordPress stores, where stock, currency, delivery information and country ownership each need their own check.'}],links:[{href:'/case-studies/speediance-au-fitness-seo/',label:'Speediance Australia case study'},{href:'/case-studies/speediance-nz-fitness-seo/',label:'Speediance New Zealand case study'},{href:'/case-studies/speediance-uk-fitness-seo/',label:'Speediance UK project review'}]},
  {id:'international-handover',eyebrow:'What You Receive',title:'The International SEO Plan',highlight:'Your Team Implements and Checks',body:['An agreed engagement can include five documents: the priority market and query map, the list of existing and proposed page owners, the regional architecture findings, the content requirements for each market, and an implementation sequence. Every action names what it depends on and how the finished work will be checked, so your developer and your content team can each pick up their part without a meeting to interpret it.','The work runs in a set order. We review current performance by country in Search Console, research each target market, recommend a structure and a page list per market, specify the technical changes, adapt the content, then check after launch and report by country.','Send the website, target countries, store or service structure and any planned launches. We will identify the decisions needed before setting scope, and the scope is confirmed in a written proposal. You do not have to build every country at once, and there is no guaranteed date for ranking in a new market.']}
 ],
 faqs:[
  {question:'Is this a translation service?',answer:'No. This service covers English-language market research, page planning, technical regional checks and measurement. Translation or native-language production would require a separately confirmed scope and qualified resources.'},
  {question:'Do we need a separate domain for every country?',answer:'Often not. Country folders on one domain are easier to maintain and share authority, while separate domains increase maintenance and can divide it. Separate domains make sense when the businesses are run separately. We assess the current domain, regional offer, existing visibility and operational needs before recommending a structure.'},
  {question:'Will hreflang make us rank internationally?',answer:'Hreflang helps search engines choose between equivalent regional or language versions of a page. It does nothing if the pages are not true equivalents. It does not replace useful content, technical accessibility, relevant authority or a viable offer, and it does not guarantee rankings.'},
  {question:'Can you work with our developer?',answer:'Yes. We prepare the regional page map and technical acceptance criteria, then review the released implementation. Responsibilities, access and release approval are agreed before any change is made.'},
  {question:'Which platforms and site set-ups do you work with?',answer:'We work with separate country domains, regional folders on one domain and a single shared catalog serving several countries. Our published multi-market work is on separate storefronts for one brand in the UK, Australia and New Zealand. On the platform side, we support Shopify, WooCommerce and WordPress stores. For each set-up we check stock, currency, delivery information and which country owns each page before recommending any change to templates or tags.'},
  {question:'Do you have a multi-country result we can check?',answer:'Yes. Our Speediance case studies report each country separately. The Australian store went from 6,550 to 10,900 organic clicks and the New Zealand store from 927 to 2,570, each over six months against the previous six. The UK page is a project review describing the work. The figures are Search Console clicks for each storefront and do not measure orders.'},
  {question:'How is this different from your country service pages?',answer:'Our country pages explain SEO delivery for a business buying in that market. International SEO addresses one business expanding its own website across several markets, including the relationships between its country pages and how each market is measured.'}
 ]
};
